WEBVTT


00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:06.270
This pinwheel pillow is a quick,
make it in a weekend fun project.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:13.380
Make yours special with variegated yarns
stripes or alternate solid colors in each

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:13.980
section.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.910
Embellish with flowers
embroidery or add a button.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:26.290
Mine is knit in plain stockinette, but you
could use any stitch pattern, even lace.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.380
We'll use the Knit it Now

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:35.019
Circle calculator tools so that you
can use any yarn and there's no math.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:41.269
Believe it or not, this pillow cover
is knit in one piece using short rows.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:46.760
The selvage edges of the knitting will be
folded around the pillow and they'll be

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.510
joined to create the center front
and the center back of the pillow.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.610
I chose to knit six wedges.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:04.320
Now I'm entering my gauge and I'm covering
an inexpensive 16 inch pillow form.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:09.860
I want mine to fit snugly, so I'm going
to knit my piece to 15 inch diameter.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.880
When I generate my pattern, I'll
get two sets of instructions.

00:01:19.485 --> 00:01:24.434
At the top is the abbreviated version two
needles and hold three times one needle

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:29.714
and hold 31 times and below
are row by row instructions.

00:01:30.645 --> 00:01:34.455
In case I get distracted while I'm
knitting, I can always look at my row

00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:35.714
counter and know where I am.

00:01:36.935 --> 00:01:39.655
Start your knitting with
waste yarn and ravel cord.

00:01:40.804 --> 00:01:45.455
Now, if you like to use a cast on comb
when you cast on, that's fine for getting

00:01:45.455 --> 00:01:45.994
started.

00:01:46.475 --> 00:01:49.475
But you're going to need to remove it
because it's going to get in the way for

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:50.405
this project.

00:01:52.024 --> 00:01:54.065
To create the pinwheel shapes.

00:01:54.065 --> 00:01:58.685
We're going to short row as instructed
in the pattern, creating more rows of

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:00.455
knitting in the middle of the piece.

00:02:01.625 --> 00:02:05.225
This area will be the
outside edge of the pillow.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.880
You need to be comfortable with putting
needles and your carriage into hold.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.109
And if you're unsure about
this, check your machine manual.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.540
I'm starting by putting two stitches
opposite the carriage into hold position.

00:02:21.079 --> 00:02:26.030
When I knit across, I'll manually wrap
that first needle in hold to prevent a

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:26.420
hole.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.329
Now for a decorative effect.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.480
Don't wrap and embrace the
eyelets that are formed.

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:38.395
Could allow a contrast in color of fabric
to peek through your pillow cover, or you

00:02:38.395 --> 00:02:41.515
could thread a ribbon or a
cord through the eyelets.

00:02:42.454 --> 00:02:45.555
Now, I don't want holes, so
I'm going to wrap every row.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:12.250
Notice how I'm moving weights towards the
center of the work as I knit to prevent

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.930
stitches from dropping off.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.829
When you get down to the last center
stitch, be sure and wrap and put the

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:56.760
needles opposite the carriage
into work and knit across.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:13.329
Then put the remaining needles
and work and knit across again.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.500
You've completed the first
wedge of your pillow,

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:22.719
I'm going to start all over and
create the rest of the wedges.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:28.500
The piece is going to look really, really
odd at this point, but hang in there.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.000
It's going to be beautiful

00:04:32.159 --> 00:04:35.159
when you're done knitting, scrap off.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:41.190
Now, even if you don't normally bind off
your scrap, I suggest you bind off for

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.270
this project.

00:04:42.995 --> 00:04:47.165
You're going to be stuffing that pillow
form into your knitting and then working a

00:04:47.165 --> 00:04:50.915
seam binding off skin
to prevent a disaster.

00:04:51.425 --> 00:04:55.115
Don't ask, just learn from
my mistake and bind off.

00:04:56.385 --> 00:05:01.005
Fold the knitting in half, and now
you can start seeing that shape.

00:05:01.905 --> 00:05:04.905
You might want to block
at this point or not.

00:05:06.625 --> 00:05:10.225
Insert the pillow form and
carefully Kitchener stitch.

00:05:10.225 --> 00:05:11.905
The opening closed.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:16.159
Done neatly, you'll never see the seam.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.270
You'll want to secure the centers of the
pillow with a running stitch and gather up

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:28.570
that hole, embellish it,
put on a button, whatever.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:34.640
Voila, you have a quick fun pillow that
can be a canvas for your creativity.

